These three new `FeeSetup` fields (`gasLimit`, `bytecodeSizeLimit`, `gasPrice`) are never propagated by the existing `toFees()` method (line 93), which uses the 3-arg `Fees` constructor. All genesis ledger and ledger-loading call sites use `toFees()`, so the FeeSettings ledger entry will silently get zeros for all gas fields regardless of configured values.
Helpful? Add 👍 / 👎

<details>
<summary>💡 Fix Suggestion</summary>

**Suggestion:** Update the `toFees()` method (lines 90-94 of `src/xrpld/core/Config.h`) to propagate the three new gas-related fields. Since `Fees` only has a 3-arg constructor, construct the `Fees` object and then set the additional fields before returning:

```cpp
[[nodiscard]] Fees
toFees() const
{
    Fees fees{referenceFee, accountReserve, ownerReserve};
    fees.gasLimit = gasLimit;
    fees.bytecodeSizeLimit = bytecodeSizeLimit;
    fees.gasPrice = gasPrice;
    return fees;
}
```

Alternatively, add a 6-argument constructor to the `Fees` struct in `include/xrpl/protocol/Fees.h` and use it here.

</details>

The problem with `SetUp()` and `TearDown()` we had before (and the guideline says about it) is when an inherited fixture is used. For example, if someone would add later a subset of tests they would have to remember to call `SetUp()` of the base class:
```cpp
struct BytecodeRunFailureTest : BytecodeRun {
  void SetUp() override {
    ByteCodeRun::SetUp();
    // init specific for BytecodeRunFailureTest
  }
};
```

While without `SetUp()` there is nothing to remember because base class is initialised automatically:
```cpp
struct BytecodeRunFailureTest : BytecodeRun {
  BytecodeRunFailureTest() {
    // init specific for BytecodeRunFailureTest
  }
};
```
